WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.012 --> 00:00:01.200
(pulsing electronic tones)

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.770
- [Narrator] More governments
are kicking people

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.340
off the internet than ever before.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.110
It usually happens during
politically-sensitive times

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:12.600
like elections, protests, and conflicts.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.770
Last year, governments
shut down the internet

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.940
more frequently than in previous years

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.470
according to a digital rights group

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:21.340
that started tracking
the practice in 2011.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.360
For instance, in India,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.920
it happened in parts of the
country more than 100 times.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.250
Authorities say shutdowns help stop unrest

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.960
and the spread of fake news

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.060
and even prevent cheating on school exams.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.300
Advocates for an open
internet say shutdowns

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.730
can cripple economies
and disrupt daily life

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.270
all while curtailing civil rights,

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.520
so here's how governments can
kick you off the internet.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.970
Usually when you type in
a URL or hit an app icon,

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.340
the Domain Name System
looks up that address

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.330
and resolves it to a string
of pre-assigned numbers.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.800
In under a second, you can
usually connect to the server

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.360
and access the site.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.460
If the government wants to
block access to a specific site,

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.470
it can request the
Internet service provider

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.900
to interfere with the DNS traffic.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.770
The provider can then redirect traffic

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.420
so it never reaches those servers.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.040
This happened last year at the
height of a political crisis

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.920
in Venezuela when opposition
leader Juan Guaido

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.267
tried to seize power from
President Nicolas Maduro.

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.690
Guaido was standing with soldiers

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:20.930
outside a military base

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.127
when he began live-streaming on Twitter.

00:01:23.127 --> 00:01:26.884
(Juan speaking foreign language)

00:01:26.884 --> 00:01:28.140
- [Narrator] He called people to rise up

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.550
against President Nicolas Maduro.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:33.580
While Guaido's call to action
ricocheted across the world,

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.260
at home, his message was blocked

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.930
for a large number of internet users.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.620
This chart shows that Venezuela's

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:41.040
state-run internet provider

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.350
restricted access to social media.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.630
The company didn't respond
to a request for comment.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.920
- We're looking at something
similar to radio censorship

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.690
or TV censorship when
authorities might bleep out

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.903
parts of a sentence or a speech.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:56.880
- [Narrator] Alp Toker is the founder

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.360
of an internet advocacy
group called NetBlocks.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.770
He says it's surprisingly
easy for the government

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.480
to turn off access to specific sites.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.810
- [Alp] This is basically an engineer

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.242
with a button on the switch.

00:02:08.242 --> 00:02:09.844
When they see something they don't like,

00:02:09.844 --> 00:02:11.700
they press the button.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.200
- [Narrator] But authorities
can be more subtle

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.750
in how they control the internet,

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:16.610
not just by blocking sites,

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:18.857
but by simply slowing down the connection.

00:02:18.857 --> 00:02:20.040
(bubble popping)
(pleasant electronic music)

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.840
This is called throttling,

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.580
so it looks like you're
apps are still running

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.810
when, in fact, the
interference is at a level

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.970
that makes using the internet painful,

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.920
so the video you're
trying to watch on YouTube

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.290
becomes very low res or it keeps loading.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:34.730
We spoke to network engineers

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.690
who monitor internet censorship,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.620
and they say that it's even hard for them

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.570
to really pinpoint the specific reason

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:42.260
behind a slow connection,

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.650
and they say that's likely
why some governments

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.363
have turned to throttling
to shrug off accountability.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:49.720
In some extreme cases,

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.590
governments can order service providers

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.160
to turn off the internet completely.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.820
This happened last year during
anti-government protests

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.823
in Iraq when the internet was cut off.

00:02:59.823 --> 00:03:02.857
(phone buzzing)

00:03:02.857 --> 00:03:05.930
- You connect the WiFi
and opened the browser,

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.930
there is nothing and the
provider sent us messages

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:13.493
we are sorry because the
government cut off the internet.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.720
- [Narrator] Ameer Hazim
is an Iraqi photographer

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.160
in Baghdad, and he's been
posting his photos on Instagram.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.240
The Iraqi Prime Minister at the time said

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.870
it's the government's
right to restrict access

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.420
when the internet is being
used to stoke violence

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.310
and conspiracy against the homeland.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.930
Because internet blackouts
disrupt critical services

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:34.940
and can hurt the economy,

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.090
authorities often target specific networks

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:39.760
and geographic areas.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.460
At the height of the protests in Iraq,

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.730
the government imposed
daily digital curfews

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:46.380
between five p.m. to seven a.m.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.780
Then during business hours,

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.640
the government ordered service providers

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.760
to turn on the connection again.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.220
We spoke to private companies
in several countries,

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.220
and they say they have little
power to refuse a request

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.550
because authorities can threaten

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:00.830
to terminate their licenses.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:02.760
Citizens have looked for workarounds

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.900
like using VPNs that connect to networks

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.560
outside of the country.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.320
Some will pay for expensive
satellite connections.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.120
Others, like Hazim, have
bought international SIM cards

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.993
from Jordan that are activated
before entering Iraq.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.640
- That helped us to keep people updated.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.150
I started during lives from Tahrir Square

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:27.000
showing the area around
and what's happening really

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.850
and how people are living

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.543
and how the government are using violence.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.290
- [Narrator] Toker says these solutions

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.320
may end up encouraging

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:37.200
more aggressive moves from the government.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.620
- The more people
attempt to get around it,

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.600
the more governments are
gonna try to switch it off.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.860
- [Narrator] The UN says
restrictions on internet access

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.300
are a violation of human rights,

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:48.960
but many countries already have laws

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.960
that make it legal to
shut down the internet

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.760
on grounds related to national security

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.190
or stopping the spread of fake news.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:57.550
- [Alp] So while this
might have started off

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.680
as authoritarian dictatorship move,

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:03.290
it gets encoded into laws.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:04.123
It becomes very difficult

00:05:04.123 --> 00:05:06.062
for the public to complain about it.

00:05:06.062 --> 00:05:09.286
(buzzing electronic tones)

